 ## Subtopics
 
 ### Voxel Array Normal Computation
-Computes normals by iterating through generated vertices, converting local space coordinates (0 to width/height/depth) to normalized coordinates (0-1), and sampling the scalar field at those normalized positions [S3, unofficial]. It includes a `FlipNormals` boolean flag to negate the computed normal if required [S3, unofficial]. A key pitfall is that normals will be all zeros in areas where the voxel values are constant [S3, unofficial].
+A variant that explicitly iterates through generated vertices, converts local space coordinates to normalized [0,1] space, and samples the voxel array directly [S3, unofficial]. It differs from general gradient estimation by hardcoding the normalization step $u = x/(W-1)$ and supporting a `FlipNormals` flag [S3, unofficial]. Choose this when working with discrete voxel arrays where vertex positions are tightly coupled to grid indices [S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(V)$, where $V$ is the number of vertices in the extracted mesh [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(V)$ to store the resulting normal vectors [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Each vertex requires six scalar field evaluations, which can be slow if the field is complex [S2, unofficial]. However, it avoids the memory bandwidth cost of storing a separate gradient texture [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement, does not require modifying the volume data structure to store gradients, and works with any scalar field function [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Sensitive to the choice of $\varepsilon$ or sample size $n$, inaccurate in flat regions where normals become zero, and requires explicit boundary handling [S2, S3, G, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When memory is constrained and the scalar field is cheap to evaluate, or when pre-computing gradients is not feasible [S2, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** When the scalar field evaluation is extremely expensive and caching is possible, or when high precision is required in regions with small-scale features [G].
+- **Time Complexity:** $O(V)$, where $V$ is the number of vertices in the extracted mesh. Each vertex requires 6 scalar field evaluations [S2, unofficial].
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(V)$ to store the resulting normal vectors [S3, unofficial].
+- **Performance:** Computing gradients at runtime is slow due to 6 extra texture fetches per sample/vertex [S2, unofficial]. Precomputing gradients into a separate texture/array is recommended for performance [S2, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:** Provides smooth, continuous normals that respect the underlying scalar field's geometry, avoiding the faceted appearance of flat shading [S1, S3, unofficial].
+- **Limitations:** Fails in constant regions (zero gradient) [S3, unofficial]. Requires access to the scalar field function or data, not just the mesh [S1, S3, unofficial].
+- **When to use:** When smooth shading is required and the scalar field is available.
+- **When not to use:** When only the mesh geometry is available (use geometric normals) or when performance is critical and precomputed gradients are not feasible [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The central difference method for gradient estimation is a standard numerical technique [G]. In the context of Marching Cubes and volume rendering, it is applied directly at vertex positions to provide per-vertex shading normals [S2, unofficial]. The specific application to Marching Cubes vertex normals is documented in open-source compute shader implementations and tutorial code [S1, S3, unofficial].
+The method originates from the standard practice of estimating surface normals for isosurfaces by evaluating the gradient of the implicit function defining the surface [S1, S2, unofficial]. It is commonly used in real-time volume rendering and Marching Cubes implementations to provide diffuse lighting calculations [S1, S2, unofficial]. The central difference scheme is a standard numerical differentiation technique [S2, unofficial].
 
